#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725251} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725250}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523091} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724969}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084390 :tutorial/content "A curve or function is defined parametrically if $x$ and $y$ are given in terms of a third parameter $t.$ This can often give rise to some interesting shapes. \n\nFor example, we can define a curve as \n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nIf we plot the curve, it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b3/b3d085ad763c368d7fafaca878c364fc13a0c83ca73b2fd17a2f349cb402a16e.png)\n\nIf we're given a parametric curve, how do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}?$ The answer is to use the chain rule. \n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange this formula into a nicer version, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{1}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use the above formula to calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for the given parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-3, \\qquad y'(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the curve is defined in terms of the parameter $t,$ the derivative is also defined in terms of $t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94a5a3d6-2fcd-522e-934f-f2bf8a8569c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1068 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084391 :tutorial/content "Remember that to find the slope $m$ of a tangent line to a function $y=f(x),$ we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = f'(x)\n$$\n\nLikewise, to find the slope $m_n$ of a normal line to a function $y=f(x),$ we take the negative reciprocal of the tangent slope:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(x)}\n$$\n\nWith parametric equations $x=x(t), \\, y=y(t),$ it works the same way. The slope of the tangent line is still the derivative of $y$ with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nLikewise, the slope of the normal line is still given by the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{x'(t)}{y'(t)}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48431a98-af5d-5fb6-a9c9-0c0a82d65d84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1071 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084467 :tutorial/content "The slope $m$ of the tangent to a parametric curve $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to find all of the locations where the tangent to the curve is horizontal. If the tangent is horizontal, this means that $m=0,$ and so provided that $x'(t)\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad y'(t) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to find the locations where the tangent is horizontal, we solve the equation $y'(t) = 0.$ We also need to double-check that $x'(t)\\neq 0$ for each solution. The final step is necessary because a slope of $\\dfrac00$ is meaningless, and we cannot deduce any information regarding the tangent slope from this expression.\n\nSimilarly, if we want to find the locations where the tangent line is *vertical,* we solve the equation $x'(t)=0,$ and we also need to check that $y'(t)\\neq 0$ for each solution.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc76ffb8-75bd-55f8-8af4-6d84235bd0cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3964 :tutorial/title "Horizontal or Vertical Tangents to Parametric Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087518} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050790}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698142} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698141}] :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/r